# PART 10 — SECURITY, DATA CONSISTENCY AND PERFORMANCE

Ensure the implementation is compatible with the existing Firebase architecture.

Important requirements:

1. Payment reconciliation must remain server-authoritative.
2. No client-supplied amount may become the trusted payment amount.
3. No client may mark its own payment as successful.
4. No client may change its own subscription status to active.
5. No client may remove its own lockdown.
6. Manual payment entry must be restricted to authorized administrators.
7. School students must not be able to change their school relationship to bypass payment enforcement.
8. Parent-child verification must remain intact.
9. School billing must never leak into a student's self-funded billing account.
10. A successful payment must never extend the same subscription twice.
11. Payment reminders must not be duplicated.
12. Existing parent progressive fee payments must continue to work.
13. Existing tutor wallet accounting must not be corrupted.
14. Existing school and parent payment histories must be correctly scoped.
15. Existing account exemptions must be preserved.

For the financial reset, preserve a backup and audit trail.

For the subscription system, avoid loading unnecessary academic data for locked users. This is important both for security and to prevent repeated Firestore reads and unnecessary loading.

Review Firestore indexes and rules if new queries or collections require changes.

